This research delves into a comprehensive evaluation of person verification methods across unimodal, bimodal, and trimodal approaches utilizing prominent public datasets and neural network architectures. Our analysis encompasses state-of-the-art features and melting strategies, allowing for flexible comparison of specific components within recognition systems to yield insightful conclusions. Additionally, we introduce a novel verification method based on the fusion of oral, facial, and textual features, which significantly enhances accuracy compared to unimodal and bimodal verification methods. Furthermore, in contrast to existing oral-facial verification systems that rely on fixed fusion strategies, we have adapted the model to function effectively in both push and pull verification scenarios, specifically for the SpeakVer dataset. Our findings highlight the following key contributions:

(a) We introduce a novel approach for person verification via the integration of oral, facial, and lexical characteristics;
(b) We present a versatile evaluation framework that accommodates various melting strategies;
(c) We conduct an in-depth comparison of melting strategies and their relative efficacy;
(d) We develop a new oral-facial verification system and demonstrate its superior performance compared to existing approaches;
(e) We highlight the importance of strategic feature selection and its adaptability to accommodate specific constraints.
